    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Listen, the last one we call it COVID-3, the $2 trillion package. Every Democrat initially voted against it. We fought for some more relief and then But reluctantly, I could speak for myself reluctantly voted for it because it just needed to meet the moment in terms of the crisis at that time. But it was not enough. And so then this week, today, the house is voting. We fought for more because, of course, the small business money ran out within just over a week. There's still more that needs to be happening in terms of putting tests in communities. And so that's what happened in terms of this last book. But it's not only not enough. It just does not meet the crisis adequately. And so I'm here fighting for what we hope will be in the next bill and we're going to fight for it. And it's a range of things that include we need to make sure that we not only have free testing which we did fight for and get, but that there's going to be free treatment. I'm fighting to say that we need to make sure that the federal government is collecting data based on race. because as we predicted, the disparities that have long existed in America, in particular for black folks on economic issues, health issues, education issues, they've now just been put under a microscope around this pandemic, and those disparities are even greater. And so I'm saying, as many of my colleagues have joined, that we will demand that the federal government collect data based on race. Why? Because we need to know so there can be an accounting for the issue, but also so that we distribute resources based on need and that we can target those resources based on need. But also I'll tell you, Mark, I'm focused not only what we need to do around testing and treatment, but looking ahead, I'm very concerned that at the point that there is a vaccine, It probably will not be in mass production. And so we also need to be putting down land of the path now to be prepared to fight, to make sure that all people have equal access to the vaccine and not just those who have access to power or resources. Because once the vaccine hits the ground, once we have a vaccine, that's going to determine who can go back to work. It's going to determine a number of factors. And we don't want to have yet in this pandemic, a second and third-class citizenry who do not have access to power, to access to wealth, and therefore reduced access or no access to what can actually save their lives and make them healthy.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Racial disparities in health, economics, and education were exacerbated by the pandemic.",
