  {
    "episode_id": 96118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Right, and so we have publicly published the findings of investigations by the Intelligence Community of the United States, which make it very clear Russia interfered in the election in the present United States in 2016. Well, here's one of the ways they did it. They basically exposed America's Achilles heel, which is the issue of race. And in that way, they targeted black voters attempting to take advantage of what was already distrust of the system, righteous distrust. And with a misinformation campaign, to do what? To turn people off, to make people say, you know, I'm not messing with that. That's just too messy. I gotta go pick up my kids, right? Well, then that effect of that is what? Voter suppression. So you've got the classic voter suppression. You've got the Russian interference. And then fast forward to today, we have the coronavirus. And so you have Republicans in Wisconsin, for example, forcing a vote because they thought people wouldn't come out and vote, right? But people stood in those lines for two and three hours. But also now we know over half a dozen people or even a dozen people have tested positive for COVID-19 because they had to stand in those lines. All of that to say, I have a bill and I am proposing what is called the Vote Safe Act, which is about three things. One, it's a $5 billion grant to stakes to encourage one vote by mail. I'm from California. We have vote by mail. It is really easy. You just get the thing in the mail. You sit down at your kitchen table with a cup of coffee, you know, call up your friends and see who you're going to vote for or look up in the paper, who you know, who's being endorsed. Vote by mail and you put it in the mailbox and it needs to be with free postage. to early voting. So my bill encourages that at least early voting is at least 20 days before the election. So people can build in around their schedule when is a convenient time to vote instead of only having the option of election day where things, you know, the car might break down, things happen. Three, safe voting, because when it comes to certain populations of folks, including everyone who celebrates those folks who walk that Edmund Pettis Bridge, We have traditions of going to the polls on election day. And we're going to be difficult to give up those traditions easily. So recognizing that, I'm saying, OK, so for people who just feel they must actually vote on election day, let's make it safe. And so that's about saying let's do curbside voting. Let's make sure that the states are required to have a standard where they say people shouldn't have to wait in line more than X number of minutes, not two and three hours. I'm also saying in my bill that we need to make access easier because for so long it has been difficult for people with voters with disabilities. So let's make access easier for them. Let's make access to voting easier for our Native American and Indigenous populations, many of whom if they live on a reservation. They don't have a post office. They don't have a postal address. And we got to make sure that we can get them there with ease, their ability to exercise their franchise to vote. So these are the things I'm proposing in my bill. But I will say this on the issue of vote by mail. We have traditions and ceremonies almost about going to vote because we know we must honor the ancestors and all that blood that was shed. We have traditions of taking the kids. We have the traditions and souls to the polls. And what I want to say to everybody is, look, vote by mail is really easy. And you can honor the ancestors by voting by mail. And so we want to also start to incorporate new traditions around how we vote. Because what the ancestors wanted most is that we vote. that our voice is heard. And so I also want to just encourage folks to think about vote by mail. Don't listen to all that nonsense that Republicans and this president is trying to spew about all, you know, there's fraud, voter fraud, people are going to manipulate the system because he spoke the quiet part out loud, by the way, and said basically the reason he doesn't want people to vote by mail is because when people vote by mail, Republicans like Donald Trump don't win.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
