  {
    "episode_id": 96118,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96118.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "I'm very concerned about it. You see what's happening in the state of Georgia, right? And that these, these, it's there's a bravado that you're hearing from politicians like the governor of Georgia, which is about about politics and the guiding force at this moment in time has got to be not politics, but public health. It's got to be public health. This is a pandemic. This is the nature of the core. This is a public health issue. And the public health issue is about a highly contagious disease, a virus, meaning people give it to each other when they're hanging out with each other. And it has resulted in an economic crisis. The cause is the public health crisis. The effect is the economic crisis in addition to thousands of people dying every day in America from it. And yes, we need to open back up the economy at some point. People got to be able to work and feed their children and pay the rent. But until we deal with the public health issue, we can't get to that place. And any reasonable, honest policymaker and elected person must speak truth even when people don't want to hear it. But that's again, Mark, why I'm here. And so many of us are fighting for these packages. I'm saying, for example, during this period of time, People who have credit cards are probably going to start buying food on those credit cards. So I have called for a suspension of interest and penalties and fees on credit cards for the next 120 days. I'm saying people are going to be late paying their bills. That's just a reality. When you have 26 million people who just lost their jobs with very little savings to rely on, people are going to be late paying their bills. So I've said, and this is my background, having been Attorney General California, that we need to suspend negative reporting to the credit score companies. Because people will be late, but suspend that negative credit reporting until we get through this. Because when we get through it, it'll take a lifetime for people to fix their credit score alerts. And what we want is at the end of this, people can get back up on their feet as quickly as possible. So these are the kinds of things that we're fighting for, acknowledging the reality of this. And the reality of this is this is awful. This is an awful situation. It is horrible. And it has highlighted what we all know. is moments like this impact the least of these who then deserve to have their government speak up for them to recognize their right to dignity, to recognize their need to be lifted up in circumstance, in situation and in spirit. And this is the fight that we have right now, where there are others who want to go and save big corporations. We need to be saved in those mom and pop businesses. Those businesses with 20 or less employees, the barber shop, the beauty shop, the flower shop. And so this is what I'm focused on.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
